#--: Overview

The declarations themselves live in #~"lib/system/net.z", which is the one
place they are written. Every definition, field, arm, generic parameter and
own-line parameter there carries its own doc comment, and #~"zls" surfaces
each on hover; the corpus runner ratchets that coverage under #~"make ci".
This page is the prose and the worked examples -- read it for how the unit
is meant to be used, and the source for the exact surface.

The #~"net" unit provides TCP sockets on localhost plus readiness
polling: the transport for tool daemons such as #~"zls --listen".
The v1 surface is deliberately small: a loopback listener,
#~"accept" / #~"connect" producing bidirectional connections that
satisfy the #~"io" #~"Reader" / #~"Writer" protocols, and
poll-based readiness with a millisecond timeout.

Accessed via qualified names only -- #~"net.listen ...",
#~"net.connect ..."; there is no unqualified re-export.

#b:"Operational notes." Connections and listeners close their
socket when they go out of scope, as a #~"File" does; call
#~".close" to close one sooner. Double closes are safe no-ops, so
a cleanup path may close unconditionally. Every fallible member returns
#~"(Result ... E: IoError)" -- the errno domain is shared with
#~"io", since socket I/O interleaves with file I/O.

#b:"Byte counts." A byte count is a value, held in the result's
own slot: the byte-count members #~"read" / #~"write" return
#~"(ResultVR u64 E: IoError)", the native side stores the bare
count with no allocation, and a match arm narrows straight to
the #~"u64":

#code language=zerolang: """
  readSome: function {c: net.Conn} is {
      buf: Bytes
      nr: c.read into: buf max: 4096
      match (
          nr
      ) case ok then {
          print "read \{nr} bytes"    # nr narrows to the bare u64
      } case err then {
          io.eprintln "read failed"
      }
  }
"""


#--: Index

#p: #b:"Types"

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"socket" -- #~"typedef i64", the system's socket: a descriptor on POSIX, a 64-bit SOCKET on Windows]
  [#*: [#> to=listener: #b:"Listener"] -- #~"class { sock: socket  open: bool } as { accept  close  pollReadable }"]
  [#*: [#> to=conn: #b:"Conn"] -- #~"class { sock: socket  open: bool } as { :Reader  :Writer  read  write  flush  close  pollReadable }"]

#p: #b:"Functions"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=listen: #b:"net.listen"] -- #~"function {port: u16} out (Result Listener E: IoError)"]
  [#*: [#> to=connect: #b:"net.connect"] -- #~"function {port: u16} out (Result Conn E: IoError)"]
  [#*: [#> to=poll-readable: #b:"pollReadable"] -- #~"function {t: this.view timeoutMillis: i32} out (ResultVR bool E: IoError)", on a Listener, a Conn, and io's File]


#--: listener


A bound, listening TCP socket on 127.0.0.1, produced by
[#> to=listen: #~"net.listen"]. #~"accept" blocks until a client
connects and returns the #~"Conn" for that client. #~"close"
releases the socket; closing an already-closed listener is a
safe no-op.

[#> to=poll-readable: #~"pollReadable"] says whether a client is
waiting, so a loop can wait for one without blocking in #~"accept".


#--: conn


One bidirectional TCP connection, produced by #~"Listener.accept"
(server side) or [#> to=connect: #~"net.connect"] (client side).
#~"Conn" declares #~":Reader" and #~":Writer" conformance, so it
plugs into any API written against the #~"io" stream protocols.

#ul:
  [#*: #~"read into: buf max: n" -- appends up to #~"max" bytes
    onto #~"buf" and returns the count actually read. A count of
    #b:"0" means the peer closed its end]
  [#*: #~"write from: bv" -- writes the view's bytes, looping
    until every byte is out; the returned count equals the input
    length on success]
  [#*: #~"flush" -- completes the #~"Writer" conformance. Socket
    writes go through directly, so nothing is buffered here and
    the call always succeeds]
  [#*: #~"close" -- shuts the connection down; double closes are
    safe no-ops]
  [#*: #~"pollReadable timeoutMillis: n" -- whether data, or the
    peer's close, is waiting]

#code language=zerolang: """
  sayHi: function {c: net.Conn} is {
      msg: Bytes
      msg.append from: 104.u8    # 'h'
      msg.append from: 105.u8    # 'i'
      bv: msg.byteView
      wr: c.write from: bv
      match (
          wr
      ) case ok then {
          print "wrote \{wr} bytes"
      } case err then {
          io.eprintln "write failed"
      }
  }
"""


#--: listen


Bind + listen on #~"127.0.0.1:<port>", backlog 16. A port another
socket holds is refused: Windows is told so with
#~"SO_EXCLUSIVEADDRUSE", and POSIX's #~"SO_REUSEADDR" only lets a
port still in TIME_WAIT be taken again. The returned #~"Listener"
accepts client connections; errors (port in use, permission denied)
surface as #~"IoError". On Windows the sockets are Winsock's, started
on first use and never inherited by a child.

#code language=zerolang: """
  lr: net.listen port: 8437
  match (
      lr
  ) case ok then {
      ar: lr.accept
      match (
          ar
      ) case ok then {
          print "client connected"
      } case err then {
          io.eprintln "accept failed"
      }
  } case err then {
      io.eprintln "listen failed"
  }
"""


#--: connect


Open a client connection to #~"127.0.0.1:<port>". The returned
#~"Conn" is ready for #~"read" / #~"write"; a refused or
unreachable port surfaces as #~"IoError".


#--: poll_readable


#~"pollReadable" is a method of each handle: a #~"Listener" (a
client waiting to be accepted), a #~"Conn" (data, or the peer's
close, to drain) and #~"io.File" (a pipe or terminal -- standard
input, say -- with data waiting; a disk file is always ready). It
answers within #~"timeoutMillis"; #~"0" polls without blocking. A
daemon multiplexing a TCP transport with stdio polls the one its
input comes through. An interrupted wait reports not-readable rather
than an error. On Windows a socket waits in #~"WSAPoll", a pipe is
asked with #~"PeekNamedPipe" and a console is waited on.

The result is the second #~"(Box T)" collapse in this unit: the
match arm binds the bare #~"bool".

#code language=zerolang: """
  waiting: function {c: net.Conn} is {
      pr: c.pollReadable timeoutMillis: 100
      match (
          pr
      ) case ok then {
          if pr then print "data waiting"
      } case err then {
          io.eprintln "poll failed"
      }
  }
"""
